- Jason says to proactively seek out customers and talk to them, and that "whenever you see something surprising, that's a signal that there's something to learn. Dig in and learn it."
- They learned a lot by asking about other products, as long as the questions were about the life of the customer. He suggests customers may be more honest precisely because it is not about you.
- From that one conversation they learned:
  - which magazine to advertise in;
  - that customers will buy two pieces of software to solve the same problem;
  - that the buyer had a credit card limit.

- Without leveraging strengths (rather than spending far more energy shoring up a weakness that still won't be great), the company won't create something great.
- Without leveraging differentiated strengths, it won't surpass competitors, will struggle to win and keep customers, and will struggle to justify profitable prices.
- Without leveraging durable differentiated strengths, success is short-lived and the company is reduced to out-spending on marketing or cutting prices until it is unprofitable.

A winning strategy states:
- which strengths the company will leverage;
- how it will side-step, not "attack," its weaknesses;
- which strengths can be leveraged for differentiated sales today;
- which long-term moats it is constructing.

OS time can go backwards (NTP, leap seconds, manual changes), repeat (CPUs are faster than clock precision) and drift far from real time. Jason's fix is to store the last-seen PT plus a counter, behind a mutex:

```go
func GetLT() {
	if PT_Current > PT_LastSeen {
		PT_LastSeen = PT_Current
		Counter = 0
	} else {
		Counter = Counter + 1
	}
	return {PT_LastSeen, Counter}
}
```

- LT orders by `PT_LastSeen`, then by `Counter`.
- Because of the counter, PT precision doesn't matter. He therefore suggests a recurring timer that refreshes a thread-safe global PT 1-4 times per second, instead of an expensive blocking OS call on every request.
- With that optimization, his Javascript implementation reached tens of millions of invocations per second on a typical laptop.
